00:15:08,078 --> 00:15:11,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Contact with that pressure, because of which I would be concentrating.

184
00:15:11,641 --> 00:15:20,449
[SPEAKER_00]: The pressure is still there, but your emphasis and your kind of weight of your attention is now directed in such a way that you won't be able to see it.

185
00:15:21,109 --> 00:15:25,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so how do I then not lose the sight of it without needing to concentrate on it?

186
00:15:26,414 --> 00:15:33,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you just have to ask yourself, because again, well, first of all... What if just something comes up and naturally distracts me?

187
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:34,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that mean I lost the sight?

188
00:15:36,062 --> 00:15:42,408
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because even then you can be aware of how you're being pressured in regard to that new thing that arose.

189
00:15:44,590 --> 00:15:50,235
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to focus on that, you don't have to kind of overly clarify that thought.

190
00:15:51,196 --> 00:15:56,120
[SPEAKER_00]: You can, in most general terms, have a background awareness of how do I feel right now.

191
00:15:57,021 --> 00:16:01,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but I forgot to ask that question because distracting things came up in my life.

192
00:16:02,402 --> 00:16:02,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but...

193
00:16:02,922 --> 00:16:04,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, does that mean I lost the touch with it?

194
00:16:05,204 --> 00:16:06,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that mean I lost the mindfulness?

195
00:16:06,905 --> 00:16:08,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Or...

196
00:16:08,746 --> 00:16:29,717
[SPEAKER_00]: If that clarity, like, that clarity might fade to some extent or the other, and that's inevitable, but what matters is, when the clarity is lost, and you kind of double down on the actions that result from the lack of perspective, then, even if you try to regain it, like, that's a difference.

197
00:16:29,737 --> 00:16:30,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I don't,

198
00:16:31,686 --> 00:16:36,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Say something comes up, you know, people's lives are busy.

199
00:16:36,647 --> 00:16:45,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Say you had a clarity about the sense fields and the peripheral, the pressure that's there underneath it and so on.

200
00:16:46,470 --> 00:16:53,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Something comes up, factual requirement to deal with things that preoccupies your attention, so you lose that clarity.

201
00:16:54,513 --> 00:16:55,413
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you're saying now,

202
00:16:56,550 --> 00:17:01,471
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I don't double down in actions of, well, I presume, unsuitable kind...

203
00:17:02,512 --> 00:17:05,713
[SPEAKER_01]: So, say, I don't double down, but I lost the clarity because I got distracted.

204
00:17:05,773 --> 00:17:07,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that mean I lost the touch with it?

205
00:17:08,533 --> 00:17:10,574
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I haven't acted out, but I haven't regained...

206
00:17:10,594 --> 00:17:11,874
[SPEAKER_01]: I haven't maintained the clarity either.

207
00:17:12,174 --> 00:17:21,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but that's not a problem in itself, because you will see, if you didn't act out and double down, if you wanted to re-establish the clarity,

208
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:23,520
[SPEAKER_00]: It would... it wouldn't be that difficult.

209
00:17:23,761 --> 00:17:40,645
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you did double down and you did just proliferate in further actions, you would see that even if you try to bring up these ideas, the whole thing is now abstract, because you're just kind of inflamed, so to speak, from all this indulgence... indulgence that you engage with.

210
00:17:40,785 --> 00:17:50,948
[SPEAKER_00]: So that neutral kind of forgetfulness will not... it's... it doesn't kind of undo

211
00:17:52,157 --> 00:17:53,659
[SPEAKER_00]: completely.

212
00:17:53,699 --> 00:17:59,366
[SPEAKER_00]: The degree of clarity that you could have built, you could restore it just by trying to bring it back.

213
00:18:01,549 --> 00:18:06,876
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you could measure, am I acting out too much?

214
00:18:07,256 --> 00:18:08,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I kind of

215
00:18:09,774 --> 00:18:11,435
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not spilling out too much.

216
00:18:12,136 --> 00:18:29,470
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... if afterwards you feel like all the stuff is just... before it was clear and now it feels kind of just abstract and just almost as if you hadn't clarified these things before and you have to keep restarting it from scratch every single time.

217
00:18:30,851 --> 00:18:31,731
[SPEAKER_00]: And you say that's because...

218
00:18:35,913 --> 00:18:37,994
[SPEAKER_01]: A person would have been acted out.

219
00:18:38,154 --> 00:18:38,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Spilling out too much.

220
00:18:38,915 --> 00:18:40,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Spilling out in unsuitable ways.

221
00:18:41,277 --> 00:18:54,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if they don't appear to them as unsuitable ways, if it got to the point where you can't just resume your clarity, then... and again, it feels like you have to start from scratch every time.

222
00:18:55,466 --> 00:18:56,927
[SPEAKER_00]: That means you were spilling out too much.

223
00:18:56,987 --> 00:19:04,332
[SPEAKER_00]: You were kind of allowing too much emphasis and focus on those factual things that you have to deal with, because that's the thing.

224
00:19:05,753 --> 00:19:09,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, it might feel like it's either or.

225
00:19:10,235 --> 00:19:13,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I either maintain this clarity or I engage with these things.

226
00:19:13,756 --> 00:19:15,536
[SPEAKER_00]: But the more you understand it, the more you realize...

227
00:19:15,556 --> 00:19:18,157
[SPEAKER_01]: But as I said, sometimes you have to engage with things.

228
00:19:18,697 --> 00:19:18,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

229
00:19:19,277 --> 00:19:23,158
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you then guarantee that you won't spill out too much?

230
00:19:24,599 --> 00:19:25,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you have to... Is there a criteria?

231
00:19:26,339 --> 00:19:28,420
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to be contained by the precepts, first and foremost.

232
00:19:30,542 --> 00:19:38,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, so if basically, okay, so then, spilling out too much would be, if things you have to deal with, require you to break precepts.

233
00:19:38,707 --> 00:19:38,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

234
00:19:39,388 --> 00:19:41,449
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be the coarsest way of spilling out too much.

235
00:19:41,689 --> 00:19:46,232
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, doesn't matter what you need to deal with, if it's against the precepts, you don't deal with it.

236
00:19:46,252 --> 00:19:46,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

237
00:19:47,152 --> 00:19:48,193
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's within the precepts?

238
00:19:49,133 --> 00:19:57,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if it's within the precepts, you, like, that's what I was going to say, you, you, you got to realize that

239
00:19:58,503 --> 00:20:09,169
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't take this kind of humongous concentrated effort to keep perspective of these things, because they're already there in your experience.

240
00:20:09,229 --> 00:20:12,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the way you're being pressured right now, no matter what you're doing...

241
00:20:12,391 --> 00:20:13,692
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pressure is already there.

242
00:20:13,852 --> 00:20:14,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

243
00:20:14,332 --> 00:20:15,793
[SPEAKER_01]: You're contained by the precepts.

244
00:20:15,973 --> 00:20:16,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

245
00:20:17,374 --> 00:20:25,919
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you might, as I said, legitimately get distracted by something, but then you have those same things to come back to, because they're still there.

246
00:20:25,999 --> 00:20:26,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

247
00:20:27,599 --> 00:20:34,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Your whole actions and your decisions that you're making, even if you were ignoring the pressure, would be based on the pressure.

248
00:20:35,161 --> 00:20:38,421
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like it's... it only exists when you look at it.

249
00:20:38,441 --> 00:20:42,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you're saying, you can... if you don't spill out, you can ignore it only so far.

250
00:20:42,542 --> 00:20:42,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

251
00:20:43,122 --> 00:20:44,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's there, pressuring you.

252
00:20:44,943 --> 00:20:45,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

253
00:20:45,463 --> 00:20:55,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you want to tune that connection with it, I guess, you could say, so that it doesn't require you to, like, pause

254
00:20:55,962 --> 00:20:57,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, things that are not against the precepts.

255
00:20:58,143 --> 00:21:04,548
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't require you to pause completely and kind of go through these mental motions to then re-establish it.

256
00:21:04,888 --> 00:21:10,211
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to see, regardless of what I'm doing, you want to find, you want to be able to find it there.

257
00:21:10,812 --> 00:21:24,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you have space to think and and plan and remember and do whatever, you definitely have space for this, because all that stuff takes even more effort than being aware of what's already there.

